SIMD emboldening model and deferred hardware checks
=================================================

Host-side model test:

    sh tests/run_retro_simd_models.sh

Or compile the deterministic model manually:

    cc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address,undefined \
       tests/mmi_embolden_equivalence.c -o mmi-embolden-test
    ./mmi-embolden-test

It models byte-level QFSRV neighbour extraction, saturated PADDUB, MMI
POR (16 bytes), and VIS1 FOR (8 bytes).  It now covers 72,000
combinations including both pitch directions, all 16 byte alignments,
short and long rows, 0..4 horizontal strength, and multiple grayscale
limits.  It also checks the untouched guard and padding bytes.

IMPORTANT: The host model cannot execute R5900 or VIS1 machine code.
The MMI/VIS1 kernels still require separate target compiler checks,
pixel-identity tests against unoptimized FreeType, and hardware
benchmarks.  Deferred target testing is described in
docs/MMI_EMBOLDEN.md and docs/VIS1_EMBOLDEN.md.

Packed conversion batch tests
=============================

The same host batch command also runs tests/convert_simd_model.c with
197,376 MMI/VIS1 packed conversion cases.  No hardware-specific code
is run by this model.

To compare the actual FreeType conversion output after building scalar
and SIMD library variants separately, build
`tests/convert_freetype_compare.c` against each variant.  It computes a
fingerprint for 16,560 deterministic cases; compare the entire lines.
Only active pixels are hashed, not potentially uninitialized row padding.

See docs/RETRO_BITMAP_CONVERT.md and docs/RETRO_SIMD_TEST_PLAN.md.

BGRA blending (common portable integer fast paths)
=================================================

The same host batch command also compiles and runs
`tests/blend_exact255_model.c`, which enumerates all 65,026 possible
numerators of an 8-bit by 8-bit product and compares 35,840 complete
BGRA rows against the original FreeType arithmetic. Both direct and
adaptive lookup-table routines are exercised in the byte model.

Compile `tests/blend_freetype_compare.c` with scalar and optimized
FreeType builds separately to fingerprint 11,520 public-API blend cases.
Because the upstream BGRA negative-pitch path is not yet implemented,
only positive-pitch blend API inputs are used in this test.

See `docs/RETRO_BITMAP_BLEND.md`.

LCD 5-tap filtering (FT_CONFIG_OPTION_SUBPIXEL_RENDERING only)
==============================================================

The host batch runner also includes tests/lcd_spans_model.c.
It exercises 165,120 deterministic horizontal and vertical
five-tap span cases with odd lengths, varying byte alignments,
multiple weights/coverages, overlapping spans and both pitch signs.
The test uses the actual portable folded implementation.

FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_LCD_SPANS provides portable folding.
FT_CONFIG_OPTION_MMI_LCD_SPANS additionally uses the R5900 PADDB
wrapping byte-add SIMD for long spans. The MMI assembly has not
been run by this host model and needs later target verification.

For LCD glyph output comparisons, enable
FT_CONFIG_OPTION_SUBPIXEL_RENDERING in both scalar and accelerated
FreeType builds, then compare retro_glyph_hash lcd/lcd-v outputs.
See docs/RETRO_LCD_SPANS.md.

4x overlap and long MONO fill (added)
=====================================

Host batch script: sh tests/run_retro_simd_models.sh

tests/overlap_mono_model.c checks the actual opt-in portable helper
against the original pixel update and monochrome span algorithms:

  - 17,408 exhaustive repeated 4x overlap correction comparisons
  - 33,408 overlap spans with a second overlapping span
  - 82,176 MONO full-span and masked-edge byte comparisons

Native R5900 and SPARC assembly execution is NOT part of this test.
The scalar path is unchanged when the new switches are disabled.

For end-to-end regression, use the same font bytes with scalar and
optimized FreeType builds and compare the following fingerprint modes:

    ./retro-glyph-hash font.ttf overlap
    ./retro-glyph-hash font.ttf mono

The overlap mode explicitly sets FT_OUTLINE_OVERLAP on an outline.
See docs/RETRO_OVERLAP_MONO.md for CPU-specific flags and limitations.

Fixed-point multiply for R5900 and SPARC32
==========================================

The host runner now includes tests/mulfix_rounding_model.c.
It compares FreeType's exact signed 64-bit FT_MulFix rounding against
the target backends' shared HI:LO word-rounding routine in 1,037,663
boundary and deterministic cases. It does NOT execute ISA assembly.

Build and run tests/mulfix_freetype_compare.c against the scalar
and target-specific FreeType builds separately; compare its full
775,517-case fingerprint for exact public FT_MulFix output.

Both FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MULFIX_R5900 and
FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MULFIX_SPARC32 require a 32-bit FT_Long ABI.
Select only the matching CPU, and inspect the compiler-generated
MULT/MFHI/MFLO or SMUL/RD %y assembly before timing it.

See docs/RETRO_MULFIX.md for assembly and ABI caveats.

Most-significant bit on R5900 and SPARC32
========================================

The host batch script also compiles tests/msb_semantics_model.c.
It checks 2,048,706 patterns against the scalar reference, including
zero, sign bit, powers, low-16 exhaustiveness and one million random
words.  It uses the actual De Bruijn helper, while R5900 PLZCW is
modeled (not executed) on the host.

For cross-compiled target builds, tests/msb_target_compare.c exercises
the internal FT_MSB interface on nonzero inputs, verifying the output
and producing a fingerprint for approximately 1.39 million inputs.
The default builtin clz is intentionally not called with zero.

FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MSB_R5900 uses PLZCW.
FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MSB_SPARC32 uses portable De Bruijn arithmetic.
Neither switch is enabled by default.  Do not select both at once.

See docs/RETRO_MSB.md for ISA semantics, limits and target checks.

32-bit fixed-point division and vector normalization
====================================================

Enable FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_DIVFIX_FAST32 to select an exact
portable C shortcut for FT_DivFix when the divisor is a power of
two or the biased numerator fits in unsigned 32 bits.
The shortcut falls back to the original function for zero divisor,
oversized quotient and other conditions where the old behavior
could differ between FT_INT64 and emulated-64-bit builds.

Host batch: tests/divfix_fast32_model.c checks 1,961,233 operand
pairs, using the actual fast-path helper and a 64-bit reference.

Compare separate scalar vs optimized 32-bit FreeType builds with:

  tests/divfix_freetype_compare.c       (993,225 public API cases)
  tests/normlen_freetype_compare.c      (300,961 internal cases)

The NormLen comparison needs STATIC linking against a matching
FreeType archive and internal headers. Its arithmetic was not
rewritten; this ensures FT_MSB backend changes do not alter the
Newton iteration output or the reported normalized length.

See docs/RETRO_DIVFIX_NORMLEN.md for configurations and caveats.

32-bit fixed-point square root (FT_INT64 only)
==============================================

Enable FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_SQRT_RESTORING to replace the existing
FT_SqrtFixed Babylonian division loop with an exact 24-step
division-free 32-bit restoring square root. The implementation is
portable unsigned C and works on both EE and SPARC32. It is off
by default and has no effect in non-FT_INT64 FreeType builds.

The deferred host batch now includes tests/sqrtfixed_restoring_model.c:
1,458,915 cases compare the real restoring helper to the original
Babylonian method and to an independent integer binary-search oracle.
The model does not execute hardware-specific instructions.

The test tests/sqrtfixed_freetype_compare.c fingerprints 512,307
FT_SqrtFixed calls from a matching STATIC FreeType archive.
Build baseline and optimized variants with the same 32-bit ABI
and FT_INT64 configuration; compare whole output lines.

See docs/RETRO_SQRTFIXED.md for the exact rounding invariant,
implementation scope and later target-performance checks.

Multiply-divide, outlines and BGRA grayscale conversions
=========================================================

FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MULDIV_FAST32 selects portable 32-bit
FT_MulDiv and FT_MulDiv_No_Round shortcuts for common factors,
safe small numerators and power-of-two divisors. The host script
builds tests/muldiv_fast32_model.c in two modes, with FT_INT64
enabled and with it unavailable (3,087,312 cases each).
An additional target test, tests/muldiv_freetype_compare.c,
hashes 699,625 public/internal API result pairs. Link it against
matching STATIC FreeType archives for the same 32-bit target.

FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_OUTLINE_TRANSFORM optionally specializes
the identity and diagonal affine transforms, general transforms
and translation of zero/single/two coordinate axes. Link
tests/outline_transform_freetype_compare.c (19,152 outlines)
and tests/outline_translate_freetype_compare.c (17,024 outlines)
separately against baseline and optimized FreeType builds.

FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_BGRA_GRAY_LUT builds a 3KB three-channel
component-square lookup only for BGRA->GRAY conversions of
at least 4096 pixels. The host runner includes the actual LUT
helper's 100,000-row exactness model. The public API test
tests/bgra_gray_freetype_compare.c checks 3240 conversions
across large/small images, row padding and both pitch signs.

All three switches are disabled by default and remain portable
with NO_ASSEMBLER. Tests and hardware benchmarks are deferred.
See docs/RETRO_MULDIV.md, docs/RETRO_OUTLINE_TRANSFORM.md and
docs/RETRO_BGRA_GRAY.md.

Adaptive MONO horizontal embolden lookup
=======================================

The option FT_CONFIG_OPTION_RETRO_MONO_EMBOLDEN_LUT uses two
256-byte per-strength tables for large packed-MONO bitmaps.
It retains the right-to-left byte update ordering and original
shift/OR semantics, with a 1024-padded-byte activation threshold.

The host batch now runs tests/mono_embolden_lut_model.c
(81,024 planned rows, including strengths 1..8 and guard bytes).

The target test tests/mono_embolden_lut_freetype_compare.c
checks 5376 FT_Bitmap_Copy / FT_Bitmap_Embolden cases on both
sides of the size threshold, both pitch signs and variable
padding. Compare fingerprints from the same scalar/optimized
FreeType revision. No tests were run while preparing the PR.

See docs/RETRO_MONO_EMBOLDEN_LUT.md.
